# sdkconfig.defaults - every line with its reason (task p0-01). This file is the configuration:
# tools/verify.sh regenerates sdkconfig from it whenever it changes, so an edit here is the build's,
# and sdkconfig itself is never committed. Facts from the Walter datasheet cite
# QuickSpot/walter-hardware walter/datasheet/walter_datasheet.tex by section.

# ---- The chip and its flash --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Walter "is based on an ESP32-S3-WROOM-1-N16R2 module" (datasheet §1 Introduction) with
# "Flash: 16MB (Quad SPI) Flash memory" (§1, Specifications).
CONFIG_IDF_TARGET="esp32s3"
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
# Quad SPI flash, read in QIO mode at the default 80 MHz: the fastest mode the WROOM-1's flash
# offers, and what QuickSpot's own examples set (walter-esp-idf examples/https/sdkconfig.defaults).
# ESP-IDF's "choose flash mode automatically" is off: it exists to tell quad from octal flash on an
# unknown board, writes DIO into the image header and costs ~900 bytes of IRAM (its Kconfig help);
# the datasheet has already told us. esptool still flashes with --flash-mode dio, which is right:
# the header and the bootloader are what put the chip in QIO.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASH_MODE_AUTO_DETECT=n
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_QIO=y
# ---- The PSRAM: one job, and it is the room of a session (task p16-05) --------------------------
# Task p0-01 left PSRAM off with the note that the first task to need it decides. This is that
# task, and what needs it is **one buffer**: the room a step of a NoiseScope session lays a
# scanner's lines down in (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §14, docs/update.md's *A session*). 64 kB of it
# where there is PSRAM, 8 192 bytes of the internal heap where there is not - which is what a unit
# in service has in one piece. Nothing this board needs in order to boot, to keep its radio, to
# update or to reach the server depends on any of it, and rail R24 holds that.
#
# The N16R2 has "2 MB of quad SPI PSRAM" (datasheet §1, Specifications); every Walter Thomas has is
# that part. The datasheet's §Ordering also lists variants with 8 MB of **octal** PSRAM, which this
# configuration does not drive: on one of those the chip does not answer in quad mode
# (esp_psram_impl_enable: "PSRAM chip is not connected, or wrong PSRAM line mode"), the room comes
# out of the internal heap instead, and the board boots and runs exactly as it does today. The
# board aborting its boot on the wrong kind - task p0-01's reason for leaving this off - is what
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_BOOT_INIT=n below makes impossible: nothing of the memory is brought up at boot,
# so there is nothing for a boot to fail at.
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
# Quad, and auto-detected type: the two defaults of the S3's Kconfig, stated because the module is
# quad and because an 8 MB octal Walter must *fail* to find a chip rather than mis-drive one.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_QUAD=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_TYPE_AUTO=y
# 40 MHz, ESP-IDF's default. 80 and 120 are offered; neither buys this firmware anything - the one
# thing in the PSRAM is a 64 kB buffer written a line at a time by a 115 200-baud UART - and the
# flash on this module is already at 80 MHz in QIO. The slowest setting is the one with the least
# to go wrong on a board that has never had its PSRAM up.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_40M=y
# **The memory is brought up by the room of a session and at no other moment.** BOOT_HW_INIT
# brings up the chip and its MSPI timing at boot, which has to be early (the timing registers are
# shared with the flash, and the functions that set them are ESP-IDF's `*_early` ones); BOOT_INIT
# - the mapping, the memory test and the heap pool - is off, so an ordinary wake maps no page of
# PSRAM and has no external pool in its heap. `platform::EspScratch::takeExternal` calls
# esp_psram_init() and esp_psram_extram_add_to_heap_allocator() at the first ask of a boot.
# This is ESP-IDF's own supported arrangement, not a trick: the help of
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_PRE_CONFIGURE_MEMORY_PROTECTION describes exactly it ("In case you wish to disable
# SPIRAM_BOOT_INIT just for delaying the PSRAM initialization and plan to use ... esp_psram_init()
# in the application code, you should still enable this config"), and
# components/esp_psram/test_apps/psram/sdkconfig.ci.psram_no_boot_init is these three lines, run
# on the esp32s3 in ESP-IDF's own CI.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_BOOT_HW_INIT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_BOOT_INIT=n
CONFIG_SPIRAM_PRE_CONFIGURE_MEMORY_PROTECTION=y
# A PSRAM that is not found at boot is a log line, not a panic: "Normally, if psram initialization
# is enabled during compile time but not found at runtime, it is seen as an error making the CPU
# panic. If this is enabled, booting will complete but no PSRAM will be available" (its Kconfig
# help). That covers the 8 MB octal variant, a board with none at all, and a chip that has stopped
# answering - all three of which must boot, run and hold their sessions in the 8 192 bytes.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND=y
# **malloc() stays internal.** The access method is "Add RAM to heap_caps allocator (malloc() stays
# internal by default)" and not ESP-IDF's default of SPIRAM_USE_MALLOC: under CAPS_ALLOC nothing
# calls heap_caps_malloc_extmem_enable(), so heap_caps_malloc_default() - which is malloc(), new
# and everything the copied library allocates - keeps asking for
# MALLOC_CAP_DEFAULT | MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL (heap/heap_caps.c). The one thing that gets external
# memory asks for it by name, with MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM, in platform/heap.cpp.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_CAPS_ALLOC=y
# And nothing static goes there. `.bss` and `.noinit` in external memory are what would make the
# image's start depend on a chip answering, and they are also what makes SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND
# unavailable - both are off, which is ESP-IDF's default, stated because rail R24 greps for them.
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_ALLOW_BSS_SEG_EXTERNAL_MEMORY is not set
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_ALLOW_NOINIT_SEG_EXTERNAL_MEMORY is not set
# No instruction or read-only data is moved into it either: this firmware's flash is fast enough,
# and code fetched from PSRAM is code that cannot run while the PSRAM is down.
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_XIP_FROM_PSRAM is not set
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_FETCH_INSTRUCTIONS is not set
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_RODATA is not set

# ---- The partition table ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# partitions.csv (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §11), checked by tools/check_partitions.py on every build.
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions.csv"
# The default offset, stated because partitions.csv and the checker both assume it.
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_OFFSET=0x8000

# ---- The console --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The module's USB-C goes to the ESP32-S3's own USB: IO19 is "USB D-" and IO20 "USB D+" among the
# internally connected pins (datasheet, Table "internal pins"); there is no USB-UART bridge. So the
# console is the USB Serial/JTAG peripheral, and UART0 (IO43/IO44, the RX0/TX0 header pins) is free.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y

# ---- Booting and staying up ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# S-6: the Walter updates itself A/B with rollback. With this on, the bootloader marks a new image
# pending-verify at its first boot and goes back to the previous slot if it sees it still pending at
# the next reset ($IDF_PATH/components/bootloader_support/src/bootloader_utility.c, the
# CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE blocks). The firmware confirms an image only after it has
# booted, spoken to the radio and reached the server (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §7).
CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=y
# The FreeRTOS tick: a thousand a second, so that **a millisecond is a tick** (task p9-01). This is
# not a timing preference, it is what makes `interfaces::Clock::delayMs` a yield at every length a
# caller asks for: `vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(ms))` is a no-op below one tick, so at the default of
# 100 Hz `platform/clock.cpp` had to busy-wait for anything under 10 ms. On 7 November 2026 that
# reset the first unit - agent-core's `JobRunner::idle` waits out the airtime budget with
# `delayMs(1)` in a loop, a 34-line `config.read` at *2 a minute, gap 20 s* made one of those waits
# about 40 s, and 40 s of `esp_rom_delay_us(1000)` starved IDLE0 past the 30 s task watchdog below
# (`tasks/phase-8/FOLLOWUPS.md`, *A busy wait below one tick still starves the watchdog*).
# `platform/clock.cpp` holds this line with a `static_assert`: lower it and the build stops.
# Allowed and recommended: the option's range is 1 to 1000 ($IDF_PATH/components/freertos/Kconfig,
# `config FREERTOS_HZ`), and every recommended configuration in ESP-IDF v5.5's
# docs/en/api-guides/low-power-mode/low-power-mode-soc.rst - DFS, auto light sleep, each of the
# chip's low-power modes - sets it to 1000, because finer scheduling is what lets a sleepy system
# drop its clock again sooner. What it costs is 900 more timer interrupts a second **while the
# Walter is awake**; deep sleep has no FreeRTOS tick at all, and this unit is asleep for all but a
# minute or two of each wake interval. Every tick-counted timeout in this firmware and in
# walter-modem 1.5.0 is written `pdMS_TO_TICKS(...)`, so all of them mean the same number of
# milliseconds at 1000 Hz as at 100; what changes is that they round to 1 ms instead of 10 (report
# p9-01 names the four places that is visible in).
CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ=1000
# The task watchdog: on, started at boot, watching both idle tasks, and a panic (so a reset and a
# core dump) when it fires. A Walter wedged in a task is a radio without its supervisor.
# Unchanged by p9-01: 30 s, panic, both idle tasks. The watchdog is what found the fault above and
# raising it would have hidden the next one.
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_EN=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_INIT=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S=30
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU0=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU1=y
# The brownout detector: on, at ESP-IDF's default threshold for the S3. The unit runs from a battery
# through a converter; a clean reset beats a flash write at a sagging rail.
CONFIG_ESP_BROWNOUT_DET=y
# A core dump to the coredump partition, in ELF form with a CRC, so a crash on a mast can be read
# at the next check-in rather than guessed at.
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_ENABLE_TO_FLASH=y
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_DATA_FORMAT_ELF=y
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_CHECKSUM_CRC32=y
# The main task's stack. Everything this firmware does runs in the main task: the console, the
# uplink's AT sequence, the agent's wake and every job. No task of ours is created. ESP-IDF's
# default of 3584 bytes overflowed at the first `lte up` on a board (23 October 2026: "A stack
# overflow in task main has been detected", then a panic reset and a core dump), which no host test
# can see. 16 KB until a measured high-water mark says otherwise; the ESP32-S3 has the RAM for it.
CONFIG_ESP_MAIN_TASK_STACK_SIZE=16384
# Logging at info: what the bench reads. A task that needs debug output raises it locally.
CONFIG_LOG_DEFAULT_LEVEL_INFO=y
CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_LOG_LEVEL_INFO=y
# **The debug level is not compiled in** (task p10-06 put it in, task p11-01 took it out again).
# The default level above is what a unit prints; CONFIG_LOG_MAXIMUM_LEVEL says which levels exist
# in the binary at all, and ESP-IDF's default - "the same as the default level" - is what this
# build takes: every ESP_LOGD is stripped at compile time, the modem library's TX and RX lines
# among them (managed_components/dptechnics__walter-modem/src: the `_transmitCmd` macro's
# `TX: ...`, `_processModemRSP`'s `RX: ...`, and the payload line at the data prompt, the last two
# inside `#if CONFIG_LOG_MAXIMUM_LEVEL >= ESP_LOG_DEBUG`).
#
# Why it is out. `CONFIG_LOG_MAXIMUM_LEVEL_DEBUG=y` brought the debug strings of every component
# that has them - 12 432 bytes of flash, which a 3 MB slot can spare - but it also brought the
# library's `_logEscapeBuffer`, 3 080 bytes of `.bss`, and `.bss` is what decides whether the
# image starts: v0.1.3 could not create its main task and every boot of it ended in
# `assert failed: esp_startup_start_app app_startup.c:86`. The whole account, with the model that
# explains it, is `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §14 and tasks/phase-11/reports/01.md; `tools/heap_at_start.py`
# is what says a build still fits. **A line added to `.bss` here is spent out of the room the main
# task's stack needs, not out of the 3 MB slot.**
#
# The trace is a build of its own again: `docs/bench.md` §13 says how to make one, and
# `trace modem on` in a build without it says so and switches nothing on.
# CONFIG_LOG_MAXIMUM_LEVEL_DEBUG is not set

# ---- The compiler -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optimise for size, asserts kept: 3 MB slots are generous today and will not stay so, and an
# assertion that fires is a core dump, which is better than the alternative. (ESP-IDF's default is
# -Og, for debugging on a desk; this firmware runs on a mast.)
CONFIG_COMPILER_OPTIMIZATION_SIZE=y
# C++17, no exceptions, no RTTI (tasks/phase-0/01): the two below are ESP-IDF's defaults, stated.
# CONFIG_COMPILER_CXX_EXCEPTIONS is not set
# CONFIG_COMPILER_CXX_RTTI is not set

# ---- Radios -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing enables WiFi or Bluetooth at boot: no component here calls esp_wifi_start or starts the
# Bluetooth controller, and the controller is not even compiled in. The WiFi driver (ESP_WIFI_ENABLED)
# is a non-optional library on this chip; it does nothing until called, and nothing calls it.
# CONFIG_BT_ENABLED is not set

# ---- walter-modem (managed component, 1.5.0) ----------------------------------------------------
# What this firmware uses of the modem is its TLS over its sockets - HTTP/1.1 is core/net's own, because
# the modem's HTTP client hands back too little of a response (task p3-02) - (docs/ARCHITECTURE
# §9). The task asked for the unused protocols - MQTT, GNSS, CoAP, BlueCherry, MOTA - to be off
# where the library's Kconfig allows; at 1.5.0 it allows none of them in practice. Each switch
# was tried in the build (report 01, deviations): MQTT or GNSS off breaks WalterModem.h, whose
# WMMQTTEventData and WMGNSSEventData name types that exist only under the switch, and
# WalterModem.cpp:4011 touches gnssHandler outside its guard; CoAP off (which takes BlueCherry)
# leaves WalterModem.cpp:615ff. and :3779ff. referring to _blueCherry and the MOTA handlers;
# MOTA off leaves WalterHTTP.cpp without esp_log.h, which it only got through MOTA's includes.
# So every library switch keeps its default, nothing of it is called, and the pin stays exactly
# 1.5.0 as the task says. MOTA compiled in is MOTA never run: it is driven by BlueCherry events and
# would need a 9 MB FAT partition named ffat that partitions.csv does not have.
# The pinout defaults - RX 14, TX 48, RTS 21, CTS 47, reset 45 - are the module's internal wiring
# (datasheet, Table "internal pins": LTE_UART0_RX/TX/RTS/CTS and LTE_RESET) and are left as they are.
# CONFIG_WALTER_MODEM_ENABLE_MQTT, _GNSS, _COAP, _BLUECHERRY, _MOTA: the library's defaults (y).
#
# One library setting is *not* left at its default (task p3-01). The library's command task
# retransmits a command that answered `+CME ERROR`, not only one that timed out
# (WalterModem.cpp:2093 sets RETRY_AFTER_ERROR, :1731-1748 sends it again), up to
# WALTER_MODEM_DEFAULT_CMD_ATTEMPTS - and `maxAttempts` is settable per command only through a
# private method, which `unlockSIM` does not use. At the default of 3, one call with a wrong SIM
# PIN sends `AT+CPIN=<pin>` three times and **blocks the card**: a PUK, the operator and a person
# at the mast. "A PIN is sent at most once per PIN value" is not something this firmware can
# promise over a library that turns one send into three, so:
CONFIG_WALTER_MODEM_DEFAULT_CMD_ATTEMPTS=1
# What it costs: a command lost to noise is not retransmitted by the library. That is the cheaper
# half - the modem's UART is a trace inside the module, with hardware flow control - and the
# retry this firmware relies on is the next wake's (components/core/uplink/failure.h). It also
# makes one blocking call's worst case CONFIG_WALTER_MODEM_CMD_TIMEOUT_MS rather than three times
# it, which is what core/uplink/policy.h sized the wake's budget against (kModemCallCeilingMs,
# held to this value by a static_assert in components/modem/include/modem/walter_uplink.h).
# CONFIG_WALTER_MODEM_CMD_TIMEOUT_MS keeps its default of 30 s: a command that needs longer than
# ten is the module's business and not the UART's, and no bench number says otherwise yet.
